\begin_layout Title
Behavioral Finance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 31, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to conventional financial and economic theory, agents are rational
 utility or wealth maximizers.
 However, there are many instances where emotion and psychology influence
 the decisions individuals, causing them to behave in unpredictable or irrationa
l ways.
 Behavioral finance is an emerging area of study that seeks to combine psycholog
ical theory with economics and finance in order to explain why people make
 irrational financial decisions.
 Many of the assigned papers dealt with the some of the underlying reasons
 and biases that cause some people to behave irrationally and often against
 their best interests.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A primary example of irrational behavior is when investors believe they
 can predict future trends and reversals using past information, even though
 the sequences they are predicting are random walks, so past outcomes provide
 no information about future outcomes.
 Bloomfield and Hales (2002) conducted experiments to test the model proposed
 by Barberis et al.
 (1998) in which investors underreact to invidual earnings surprises on
 average, but overreact to long-term trends in earnings.
 The two experiments they constructed strongly support the existence of
 regime-shifting beliefs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The type of information investors receive can also influence their behavior.
 With the rise of the Internet, investors today can now quickly acquire
 a large amount of financial information.
 However, more information does not necessarily lead to more knowledge.
 Kirchler, Maciejovsky, and Weber (2005) investigate the impact of irrelevant
 information on trading behavior.
 The information was symmetrically provided in either a positive or negative
 frame, and it was irrelevant to the decision.
 The authors find that while trading prices and volume remained unaffected,
 there was a particular change in trading pattern.
 Also, the experiment provided support for the disposition effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are other significant factors that could influence individual portfolio
 allocations.
 Bias is one factor that could negatively impact the decisions of financial
 investors.
 Biais and Weber (2009) formulate a theoretical model and experiment to
 study the effect hindsight bias has on risk pereption and investment performanc
e.
 Note that hindsight-biased agents fail to update their prior beliefs after
 observing new information.
 The authors find that hindsight bias reduced volatility estimates, and
 biased agents have lower performance.
 Fellner (2009), on the other hand, primarily focuses on the illusion of
 control.
 Participants in the experiment demonstrate illusion of control when they
 are in charge of the chance move.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

All of the papers previously mentioned are concerned with individual psychologic
al behaviors.
 However, none of them focus on psychological behaviors which could be influence
d by group dynamics.
 For example, what are the effects of group decision making on financial
 decision? Also, how do peer effects and group communication impact individual
 financial decision making? I believe that group biases could have a significant
 effect on financial decisions, which deviate from rational behavior.
 Moreover, does a person's educational or work background have a noteworthy
 effect on financial behavior? Would professional financial investors make
 better decisions than individuals with an extensive math and/or statistical
 background?
\end_layout

